  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Intermittent neurologic episodes over ~1.5–2 months.\n- Symptoms:\n  - Right-hand pins-and-needles and numbness with subjective right arm weakness; initially lasted 3–4 days then resolved; now occurring more frequently (reported up to a couple of times per day).\n  - Concurrent right leg weakness during episodes; colleague noted abnormal gait only during episodes.\n  - Difficulty holding a cup and slightly harder to drink during episodes; sensation of something in the throat during episodes.\n  - General fatigue, intermittent nausea, low motivation.\n  - No current symptoms at time of consult.\n  - Denies speech difficulty; no definite visual changes (wears glasses).\n- Past medical history: None reported.\n- Medications/allergies: Aspirin 75 mg daily (self-initiated for primary prevention); allergic to penicillin.\n- Family/social history: Father had carotid surgery to reduce stroke risk; patient is a 32-year-old non-smoker, works as a medical doctor (plastic surgery), lives with wife.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: None recorded in transcript (video lost during call; no in-person exam).\n- Labs/imaging: Prior screening after father's surgery—normal BP, cholesterol, and diabetes check (timing not specified). No current labs or imaging yet.\n- Plan from GP: Urgent referral to TIA clinic for carotid ultrasound and possible brain imaging; ER precautions if symptoms recur and persist.",
